        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f58bd531-548f-57d7-83a2-1db79ee1b1cf",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Roles: Deputy Führer\n- Events: Hess's May 1941 flight, World War II, Nuremberg convictions, Hess's imprisonment and suicide\n- Topics: Historians' debate\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- In May 1941, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland to try to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch was responsible for reporting Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's flight have been debated by historians.\n- After Rudolf Hess was taken prisoner, he was convicted of crimes against peace.\n- Rudolf Hess served a life sentence until his suicide.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "098d7e57-8ce9-5e36-bf81-c38f2538005f",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.",
